Install kubectl
Objective
Install kubectl, the Kubernetes command-line tool used to communicate with and manage Kubernetes clusters.

Why Do We Need kubectl?
Think:
docker  -> Docker CLI
kubectl -> Kubernetes CLI

Examples:
kubectl get pods
kubectl get nodes
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Without kubectl:
DevOps Engineer ❌ Kubernetes
Installation Steps
Step 13.1 - Download kubectl
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
What Happens?
This downloads the latest stable kubectl binary from Kubernetes official servers.

Step 13.2 - Verify File Downloaded
ls -l kubectl
Expected:
-rw-r--r-- ...
kubectl
Notice:
No execute permission yet
Step 13.3 - Make Executable
chmod +x kubectl
Why?
Adds:
Execute Permission
so Linux can run it as a program.

Step 13.4 - Verify Permission
ls -l kubectl
Expected:
-rwxr-xr-x
Notice:
x = execute permission
Step 13.5 - Move To System Path
sudo mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/
Why?

Instead of:
./kubectl
you can run:
kubectl
from anywhere.

Step 13.6 - Verify Installation
kubectl version --client

Expected:
Client Version: v1.xx.x
Interview Questions
What is kubectl?

kubectl is the Kubernetes command-line interface used to interact with and manage Kubernetes clusters.

Why move kubectl to /usr/local/bin?
/usr/local/bin is part of the system PATH, allowing the command to be executed from any directory.

What does chmod +x kubectl do?
It adds execute permission to the kubectl binary, allowing Linux to run it as a program.
